#
# Link time optimisation
#
# Opt in, not composed by default:
#
#       sh launch lto
#       sudo sh build.sh arch/x64 debug_none serial lto
#
# The kernel's LTO is Clang's and only Clang's -- there is no GCC path -- so
# this is not a flag, it is the whole toolchain. Everything gets compiled by a
# different compiler with different codegen, and the build host needs clang,
# lld and the llvm binutils rather than gcc and binutils.
#
# It works. It builds clean, boots, brings the compositor up and claims the
# cursor plane, and the image comes out 258 KB smaller -- 8,373,248 down to
# 8,115,200, about 3%.
#
# What it does not do is show up in the number this project measures. Eight
# paired runs under KVM, 300 pointer events each: 25.1 us against gcc's 25.0.
# Dead even, and the two worst runs of the sixteen were both this build.
#
# So it stays opt in. Switching the entire kernel to another compiler for
# three percent of image size and no measured latency, on a config that has
# only ever been validated on one machine, is the user's call rather than a
# default -- and it wants a boot on real hardware before it becomes one.
# Kconfig marks it EXPERIMENTAL, which is fair.
#
# One warning is expected and benign: ld.lld reports HUF_readCTable in the
# zstd decompressor using 1288 bytes of stack against a 1280 limit.
#

#> make_flags LLVM=1

CONFIG_LTO_CLANG_THIN=y
CONFIG_LTO_NONE=n
